From d5e07460c432c3291e640bc9a0b111dcb62c6ea6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mark Harfouche <mark.harfouche@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 14:38:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Fail the run when the TensorFlow tests are skipped wholesale

The 162 Keras pose-net tests are skippable twice over, and both skips are
silent:

* each of the five modules starts with `pytest.importorskip("tensorflow")`,
  so in an environment without TensorFlow all 162 disappear and pytest exits
  0. The conda recipe runs no pytest at all, so nothing would notice.

* eager execution is per-process global state that only ever flips one way,
  so anything that builds a TF1 graph earlier in the same run makes
  `ensure_eager_execution` raise for the rest of the session, and each
  module's autouse fixture turns that into a skip. Building one placeholder
  before calling `pytest.main` on the five modules gives `157 skipped`, exit
  code 0.

Both shapes have already happened during this port -- once a fixture left
eager disabled and silently skipped 49 tests while reporting green, once a
subprocess imported the installed package instead of the tree under test --
and both times the run was green.

`tests/conftest.py` now records, per tracked module, how many tests were
collected and how many actually reached their call phase, and fails the
session if a module was collected but contributed nothing. The two cases
above become, respectively, five "skipped while being imported" lines and
four "all N collected tests were skipped" lines, plus exit code 1.

One session-scoped guard rather than a change to each module's eager
fixture: a fixture that raised instead of skipping would catch the ordering
half only and would say nothing about modules that were never imported, and
it would have to be repeated in every module that is added later. The
counting is done with `trylast=True` so that `-k`/`-m` deselection is not
mistaken for a mass skip, and `DLC_ALLOW_SKIPPED_TF_TESTS=1` waives the
guard for a run that deliberately has no TensorFlow -- it has to be typed,
which is the point.

The sdist ships no `tests/conftest.py`; upstream's git tree does, and there
this file's hooks belong at the end of it.
---
 tests/conftest.py | 126 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 126 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 tests/conftest.py

diff --git a/tests/conftest.py b/tests/conftest.py
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..9765d5c
--- /dev/null
+++ b/tests/conftest.py
@@ -0,0 +1,126 @@
+#
